\f0\fs24 \cf0 Core question of the model: How do increased bacterial persistence and mutation rates, individually and in combination, affect the likelihood and timing of the emergence of genetic antibiotic resistance during clinical treatment?\
\
Rephrased: Can the coexistence of high persistence (phenotypic tolerance) and elevated mutation rates in bacterial populations synergistically accelerate the evolution of multi-step genetic resistance under realistic clinical treatment conditions, including antibiotic pharmacokinetics and host immune response?\
\
Tests: \
- Whether survival advantage of persisters during treatment is sufficient to promote resistance\
- Whether mutation rates in persisters play a meaningful role \
- How these traits together determine treatment success or failure by enabling resistant populations to rise above a clinically relevant threshold (10^8 cells)\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 current model addresses the interplay between persistence and mutation rates in a \strokec2 non-spatial, well-mixed infection context with periodic antibiotic treatment\strokec2 . Extending it to include \strokec2 tolerance\strokec2 , \strokec2 temporal control of treatment\strokec2 , and \strokec2 spatial heterogeneity\
\
\

\f1\b Balance between persistence and tolerance
\f0\b0 \
Current limitation:\strokec2  The model explicitly handles 
\f2\i \strokec2 persistence
\f0\i0 \strokec2  (phenotypic switching to a dormant, drug-insensitive state), but not 
\f2\i \strokec2 tolerance
\f0\i0 \strokec2 , which is a \strokec2 population-wide reduction in killing rate\strokec2 , often due to slowed metabolism.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 - Under what conditions 
\f1\b does tolerance outperform persistence
\f0\b0 , or vice versa, in promoting survival and resistance?\
- Can we identify a critical trade-off or optimal ratio between tolerance and persistence for different antibiotic regimens?\
- Do certain antibiotics or dosing schedules selectively favor one strategy over the other?\
\
Possible additions: \
- Introduce tolerant subpopulation (distinct from persisters; reduced killing rate, but still growing)\
- Adjust pharmacodynamic terms; tolerance affects killing slope (Hill coefficient) or max kill rate\
- Allow evolution of tolerance traits (eg upregulation of stress response genes)\
\

\f1\b Temporal optimization of treatment
\f0\b0 \
- Current limitation: fixed-interval dosing\
- Can dynamically timed treatments (early vs delayed, pulsed v continuous) exploit weaknesses in persistence/tolerance balance?\
- Can delaying treatment allow immune response to reduce persisters before antibiotics are used?\
- What timing strategies minimize the emergence of resistance in mixed tolerant/persistent populations?\
\
Possible additions:\
- Time-dependent treatment schedules\
- Feedback controls (adaptive dosing based on immune response or bacterial load)\
- Compare outcomes like time to resistance, total bacterial burden, clearance success\
\

\f1\b Spatial structure
\f0\b0 \
- Current limitation: assumes well-mixed environment (no spatial variation)\
- How do spatial gradients affect persistence/tolerance/resistance?\
- Are certain infection sites more prone to resistance because of spatial refuges?\
- Can spatially targeted treatments reduce resistance?\
\
Possible additions:\
- 
\f1\b Spatial structure
\f0\b0  via:\
 	- Compartmental models (eg lung, bloodstream, tissue)\
	- PDEs to simulate antibiotic diffusion or diffusion of immune effectors and bacteria\
	- Agent-based modeling if discrete spatial resolution is needed\
- Include differential antibiotic penetration and immune access in compartments\
- Track migration or diffusion of bacteria across compartments\
\

\f1\b Scenario testing:
\f0\b0 \
- Emergence of resistance under:\
	- high tolerance v high persistence\
	- early v delayed antibiotic dosing\
	- nfections in peripheral tissues v. bloodstream\
